"disable this behavior with "
"the :attr:`~canaille.backends.sql.configuration.SQLSettings.AUTO_MIGRATE` "
"setting. Migrations are not automatically applied with the use of the CLI "
"though."
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/database/sql.rst:27 9097d704ec16431a812e072c85fa127e
msgid ""
"Migrations are done with :doc:`flask-alembic <flask-alembic:use>`, that "
"provides a dedicated CLI to manually tune migrations. You can check "
"the :doc:`flask-alembic documentation <flask-alembic:index>` and the "
"``canaille db`` command line if you are in trouble."
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:2 1213e2d4e4484e36bb0ccb21c10d7eb2
msgid "Deployment"
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:4 a8e84a513f894b07a06d6e4053f1bf06
msgid ""
"This page details what to install on your server to make a production-ready "
"environment for Canaille."
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:7 a7f2a4f05de54e57bf253e706cba8c1a
msgid "Application service"
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:9 10790aea4da44421966220622d130c43
msgid ""
"After having finished Canaille :doc:`installation <install>` you have to run "
"it in a WSGI application server. Several application servers are available, "
"like `Gunicorn`_, `uWSGI`_, `uvicorn`_ or `Hypercorn`_ for example."
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:12 f89520983b994c34b39b775f0cbef91c
msgid ""
"Canaille comes with a light Hypercorn integration but you can use it with "
"any application server. The WSGI entry-point to configure in your server of "
"choice is ``canaille:create_app``."
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:16 1544720f7f274c60a399f7c606352d22
msgid "Hypercorn integration"
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:18 1b92d575764c4b83a721e0b0d2cbd5be
msgid ""
"To run the integrated Hypercorn application server, you can simply run "
"the :ref:`cli_run` command. The hypercorn integration is embedded in the "
"Canaille binaries. However if you use the Canaille Python package, you will "
"need the ``[server]`` extra (for instance with ``pip install "
"canaille[server]`` for Hypercorn to be available."
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:22 3fc942a0096b41f4a9ca1253e5a21aa8
msgid "Running the Canaille run command"
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:29 0465bf27f590465c9dc194df28dccb04
msgid ""
"By default it runs on the port `8000` of localhost, this could be enough but "
"you might want to customize it a little bit more. Fortunately Hypercorn "
"provides a :doc:`configuration documentation <hypercorn:how_to_guides/"
"configuring>` to adjust it to your needs. You can tweak Hypercorn in the "
"main Canaille configuration file, at the :class:`CANAILLE_HYPERCORN "
"<canaille.hypercorn.configuration.HypercornSettings>` namespace, or by "
"passing :ref:`CLI parameters <cli_run>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:33 26c94822fdef492eb75483aecde341a9
msgid "Running the Canaille run command with parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:39 cef23e73dbb844d58e3c575b1f65869b
msgid "Proxy configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:41 8c16245d97594d6c8e288478d3c24d28
msgid ""
"When running Canaille behind a reverse proxy like Nginx or Caddy, you should "
"configure the  setting to properly handle forwarded headers. With the "
"``CANAILLE_HYPERCORN__PROXY_MODE`` env var, or with the :attr:`PROXY_MODE "
"<canaille.hypercorn.configuration.HypercornSettings.PROXY_MODE>` "
"configuration setting in the ``[CANAILLE_HYPERCORN]`` namespace, set the "
"value to ``legacy`` for traditional ``X-Forwarded-*`` headers (the most "
"common), or ``modern`` for RFC 7239 ``Forwarded`` headers. You can also "
"adjust :attr:`~canaille.hypercorn.configuration.HypercornSettings.PROXY_MODE` "
"to match the number of trusted proxies between the client and your "
"application (defaults to 1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../howtos/deployment.rst:47 49c650ae6fee4b868da87abf89a6c945
