msgid ""
"Without :envvar:`CANAILLE_CONFIG`, Canaille reads ``canaille.toml`` in the "
"current directory if it exists. Applications that embed Canaille can pass "
"another file to :func:`~canaille.create_app` with ``config_file``, or "
"``config_file=False`` to read no configuration file."
msgstr ""

#: ../references/configuration.rst:35 6684707c07384eb69360f7ccecdc2620
msgid "Environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../references/configuration.rst:37 4e540b662a684eff83e595b569af776d
msgid ""
"In addition, parameters that have not been set in the configuration file can "
"be read from environment variables. The way environment variables are parsed "
"can be read from the `pydantic-settings documentation <https://"
"docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/pydantic_settings/#parsing-environment-"
"variable-values>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../references/configuration.rst:42 ae0e69c3e0da43088f3c9da0fd262875
msgid ""
"For environment vars, the separator between sections and variables is a "
"double underscore: ``__``. For instance, the ``NAME`` var in the "
"``CANAILLE`` section shown above is ``CANAILLE__NAME``."
msgstr ""

#: ../references/configuration.rst:46 35f443d52bad4971a73914b204fa6004
msgid "Environment file"
msgstr ""

#: ../references/configuration.rst:48 366366b5405142b6a1289d852a8e11b0
msgid ""
"Any environment variable can also be written in an environment file, which "
"path should be passed in the ``CANAILLE_ENV`` environment variable. For "
"instance, set ``CANAILLE_ENV=.env`` to load a ``.env`` file."
msgstr ""

